WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.810
Suppose I give you two different lists of
numbers or maybe two different functions and

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:08.340
I ask you to think of all the ways you might
combine those two lists to get a new list

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.280
of numbers or combine the two functions to
get a new function.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.740
Maybe one simple way that comes to mind is
to simply add them together term by term.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.660
Likewise, with the functions, you can add
all the corresponding outputs.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.740
In a similar vein, you could also multiply
the two lists term by term and do the same

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.500
thing with the functions.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.030
But there's another kind of combination just
as fundamental as both of those but a lot

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.149
less commonly discussed known as a Convolution.

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:38.260
But unlike the previous two cases, it's not
something that's merely inherited from an

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.399
operation you can do to numbers.

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:45.100
It's something genuinely new for the context
of list of numbers or combining functions.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.750
They show up all over the place, they are
ubiquitous in image processing.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.789
It's a core construct in the theory of probability,
they're used a lot in solving differential

00:00:53.789 --> 00:00:58.870
equations and one context where you've almost
certainly seen it if not by this name is multiplying

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.879
to polynomials together.

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:05.290
As someone in the business of visual explanations,
this is an especially great topic.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:10.640
Because the formulaic definition in isolation
and without context can look kind of intimidating.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.860
But if we take the time to really unpack what
it's saying and before that, actually motivate

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.110
why you would want something like this, it's
an incredibly beautiful operation.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.740
And I have to admit I actually learned a little
something while putting together the visuals

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.080
for this project.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.610
In the case of convolving two different functions,
I was trying to think of different ways you

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.640
might picture what that mean.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.820
And with one of them, I had a little bit of
an aha moment for why it is that normal distributions

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.710
play the role that they do in probability.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.470
Why it's such a natural shape for a function
but I'm getting ahead of myself, there's a

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.930
lot of setup for that one.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:46.250
In this video, our primary focus is just going
to be on the discrete case and in particular

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.210
building up to a very unexpected but very
clever algorithm for computing these, and

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:58.970
I'll pull out the discussion for the continuous
case into a second part.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.780
It's very tempting to open up with the image
processing examples since they're visually

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.460
the most intriguing but there are couple bits
of finickiness that make the image processing

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.190
case less representative of convolutions overall.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.060
So instead, let's kick things off with probability.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.530
And in particular, one of the simplest examples
that I'm sure everyone hears thought about

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.000
at some point in their life.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.310
Which is rolling a pair of dice and figuring
out the chances of seeing various different

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.310
sums.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:23.780
And you might say not a problem.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:24.780
Not a problem.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.780
Each of your two dice has six different possible
outcomes, which gives us a total of 36 distinct

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.930
possible pairs of outcomes.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:36.600
And if we just look through them all, we can
count up how many pairs have a given sum.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.800
And arranging all the pairs in a grid like
this, one pretty nice thing is that all of

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.030
the pairs that have a constant sum are visible
along one of these different diagonals.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:50.420
So simply counting how many exist on each
of those diagonals will tell you how likely

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.370
you are to see a particular sum.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.190
And I'd say, very good, very good.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:59.459
But can you think of any other ways that you
might visualize the same question?

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:03.819
Other images that can come to mind, to think
of all the distinct pairs that have a given

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:05.099
sum?

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:08.140
And maybe one of you raises your hand and
says, 'Yeah, I've got one.'

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.290
Let's say you picture these two different
sets of possibilities each in a row, but you

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.239
flip around that second row.

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:19.629
That way, all of the different pairs which
add up to seven line up vertically like this...

00:03:19.629 --> 00:03:23.360
And if we slide that bottom row all the way
to the right, then the unique pair that adds

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.810
up to two, the snake eyes, are the only ones
that align.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:31.129
And if I shlunk that over one unit to the
right, the pairs which align are the two different

00:03:31.129 --> 00:03:33.159
pairs that add up to three.

00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:37.530
And in general, different off set values of
this lower array, which remember, I had to

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:45.220
flip around first, reveal all the distinct
pairs that have a given sum.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.370
As far as probability questions go, this still
isn't especially interesting because all we're

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.860
doing is counting how many outcomes there
are in each of these categories.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.950
But that is with the implicit assumption that
there's an equal chance for each of these

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:58.579
faces to come up.

00:03:58.579 --> 00:04:02.310
But what if I told you I have a special set
of dice that's not uniform.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.990
Maybe the blue die has its own set of numbers
describing the probabilities for each face

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.959
coming up and the red die has its own unique
distinct set of numbers.

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:14.549
In that case if you wanted to figure out,
say, the probability of seeing a two, you

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:19.180
would multiply the probability that the blue
die is a 1X the probability that the red die

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.220
is a one.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.919
And for the chances of seeing a three, you
look at the two distinct pairs where that's

00:04:23.919 --> 00:04:29.330
possible and again multiply the corresponding
probabilities and then add those two products

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:30.330
together.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.690
Similarly, the chances of seeing a four involves
multiplying together three different pairs

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.520
of possibilities and adding them all together.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.770
And in the spirit of setting up some formulas,
let's name these top probabilities "A1, A2,

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.660
A3" and so on and name the bottom ones "B1,
B2, B3" and so on.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.880
And in general, this process where we are
taking two different arrays of numbers, flipping

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.389
the second one around and then lining them
up at various different off set values, taking

00:04:55.389 --> 00:04:59.389
a bunch of pairwise products and adding them
up, that's one of the fundamental ways to

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:03.200
think about what a convolution is.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:09.680
So, just to spell it out a little more exactly-
through this process, we just generated probabilities

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.090
for seeing two, three, four, on and on up
to 12.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:17.550
And we got them by mixing together one list
of values, A and another list of values, B.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:23.130
In the lingo, we'd say the convolution of
those two sequences gives us this new sequence;

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:27.880
the new sequence of 11 values, each of which
looks like some sum of pairwise products.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.240
If you prefer, another way you could think
about the same operation is to first create

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.190
a table of all the pairwise product and then
add up along all these diagonals.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:41.729
Again, that's a way of mixing together these
two sequences of numbers to get us a new sequence

00:05:41.729 --> 00:05:43.300
of eleven numbers.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.350
It's the same operation as the sliding windows
thought, just another perspective.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:51.040
Putting a little notation to it, here's how
you might see it written down: The convolution

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.070
of A and B, denoted with this little asterisk,
is a new list.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:06:00.259
And the Nth element of that list looks like
a sum and that sum goes over all different

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:05.180
pairs of indices, I and J, so that the sum
of those indices is equal to N.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.950
It's kind of a mouthful but for example, if
N was six, the pairs we're going over are

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:14.270
one and five, two and four, three and three,
four and two, five and one.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.169
All the different pairs that add up to six.

00:06:16.169 --> 00:06:20.370
But honestly, however you write it down, the
notation is secondary in importance to the

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.130
visual you might hold in your head for the
process.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:27.270
Here, maybe it helps to do a super simple
example where I might ask you, what's the

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:31.539
convolution of the list one, two, three with
the list four, five, six?

00:06:31.539 --> 00:06:35.910
You might picture taking both of these lists,
flipping around that second one and then starting

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.270
with its slid all the way over to the left.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:41.990
Then the pair of values which align are one
and four, multiply them together and that

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:44.200
gives us our first term of our output.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:49.669
Slide that bottom array one unit to the right,
the pairs which align are 1, 5, 2 and 4.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:53.080
Multiply those pairs, add them together and
that gives us 13.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:55.110
The next entry in our output.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:07:02.199
Slide things over once more and we'll take
1X6+2X5+3X4 which happens to be 28.

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:08.930
One more slide and we get 2X6+3X5 and that
gives us 27 and finally the last term looks

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:10.790
like 3X6.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:14.539
If you'd like, you can pull up whatever your
favorite programming languages and your favorite

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:19.080
library that includes various numerical operations
and you can confirm I'm not lying to you.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:24.310
If you take the convolution of 1, 2, 3 against
4, 5, 6, this is indeed the result that you'll

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.310
get.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:30.139
We've seen one case where this is a natural
and desirable operation adding up to probability

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:31.460
distributions.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:34.110
And another common example would be a moving
average.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:38.610
Imagine you have some long list of numbers
and you take another smaller list of numbers

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:39.880
that all add up to one.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.910
In this case, I just have a little list of
five values and they're all equal to one fifth.

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:49.360
Then if we do this sliding window convolution
process and kind of close our eyes and sweep

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.919
under the rug what happens at the very beginning
of it.

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:56.509
Once our smaller list of values entirely overlaps
with the bigger one, think about what each

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:59.069
term in this convolution really means.

00:07:59.069 --> 00:08:04.569
At each iteration, what you're doing is multiplying
each of the values from your data by one fifth

00:08:04.569 --> 00:08:06.349
and adding them all together.

00:08:06.349 --> 00:08:11.039
Which is to say you're taking an average of
your data inside this little window.

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:15.380
Overall, the process gives you a smooth out
version of the original data.

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:18.900
And you could modify this starting with a
different little list of numbers and as long

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.889
as that little list all adds up to one, you
can still interpret it as a moving average.

00:08:22.889 --> 00:08:27.669
In the example shown here, that moving average
would be giving more weight towards the central

00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:28.669
value.

00:08:28.669 --> 00:08:33.459
This also results in a smooth out version
of the data.

00:08:33.459 --> 00:08:37.479
If you do kind of a two dimensional analog
of this, it gives you a fun algorithm for

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:39.460
blurring a given image.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:43.770
And I should say the animations I'm about
to show are modified from something I originally

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:48.950
made for part of a set of lectures I did with
the Julia Lab at MIT for a certain open courseware

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:51.630
class that included an image processing unit.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:54.550
There we did a little bit more to dive into
the code behind all of this.

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:56.700
So if you're curious, I'll leave you some
links.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.910
But focusing back on this blurring example.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:03.390
What's going on is I've got this little 3X3
grid of values that marching along our original

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:04.390
image.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:07.050
And if we zoom in, each one of those values
is one ninth.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:11.890
And what I'm doing at each iteration is multiplying
each of those values by the corresponding

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.940
pixel that it sits on top of.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:18.100
And of course in computer science, we think
of colors as little vectors of three values

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:20.670
representing the red, green and blue components.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:24.640
When I multiply all these little values by
one ninth and I add them together, it gives

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.880
us an average along each color channel and
the corresponding pixel for the image on the

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.010
right is defined to be that sum.

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:36.270
The overall effect as we do this for every
single pixel on the image, is that each one

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:40.430
kind of bleeds into all of its neighbours
which gives us a blurrier version than the

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:41.930
original.

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:46.649
In the lingo, we'd say that the image on the
right is a convolution of our original image

00:09:46.649 --> 00:09:48.310
with the little grid of values.

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:52.970
Or more technically, maybe I should say that
it's the convolution with a 180 degree rotated

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:54.960
version of that little grid of values.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.470
Not that it matters when the grid is symmetric
but it's just worth keeping in mind that the

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:02.709
definition of a convolution, as inherited
from the pure math context, should always

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:05.970
invite you to think about flipping around
that second array.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.880
If we modify this slightly, we can get a much
more elegant blurring effect by choosing a

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.430
different grid of values.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:16.149
In this case, I have a little 5X5 grid but
the distinction is not so much its size.

00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:19.680
If we zoom in, we notice that the value in
the middle is a lot bigger than the value

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:20.839
towards the edges.

00:10:20.839 --> 00:10:25.570
And where this is coming from, is they're
all sampled from a bell curve known as a Gaussian

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:26.570
distribution.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:30.860
That way, when we multiply all of these values
by the corresponding pixel that they're on

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:35.360
top of, we're giving a lot more weight to
that central pixel and much less towards the

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:36.839
ones out at the edge.

00:10:36.839 --> 00:10:41.420
And just as before, the corresponding pixel
on the right is defined to be this sum.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.390
As we do this process for every single pixel,
it gives a blurring effect which much more

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:50.000
authentically simulates the notion of putting
your lens out of focus or something like that.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.860
But blurring is far from the only thing that
you can do with this idea.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:57.960
For instance, take a look at this little grid
of values which involves some positive numbers

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:02.310
on the left and some negative numbers on the
right, which I'll color with blue and red

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:03.730
respectively.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:07.700
Take a moment to see if you can predict and
understand what effect this will have on the

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:10.760
final image.

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:14.990
So in this case, I'll just be thinking of
the image as grey scale instead of coloured.

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:18.519
So each of the pixels is just represented
by one number instead of three.

00:11:18.519 --> 00:11:22.180
And one thing worth noticing is that as we
do this convolution, it's possible to get

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:23.180
negative values.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:27.270
For example, at this point here if we zoom
in, the left half of our little grid sits

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.960
entirely on top of black pixels which would
have a value of zero.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.630
But the right half of negative values all
sit on top of white pixels which would have

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:36.260
a value of one.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:40.510
So when we multiply corresponding terms and
add them together, the result will be very

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:41.510
negative.

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:44.670
And the way I'm displaying this with the image
on the right, is to color negative values

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.130
red and positive values blue.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:50.640
Another thing to notice is that when you are
on a patch that's all the same color, everything

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:55.470
goes to zero since the sum of the values in
our little grid is zero.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.470
This is very different from the previous two
examples where the sum of our little grid

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:03.820
was one, which let us interpret it as a moving
average and hence a blur.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:08.000
All in all, this little process basically
detects wherever there is variation in the

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.380
pixel value as you move from left to right.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:14.820
And so it gives you a kind of way to pick
up on all the vertical edges from your image.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:20.820
And similarly, if we rotated that grid around
so that it varies as you move from the top

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:24.589
to the bottom, this will be picking up on
all the horizontal edges.

00:12:24.589 --> 00:12:28.949
Which in the case of our little pie creature
image, does result in some pretty demonic

00:12:28.949 --> 00:12:30.639
eyes.

00:12:30.639 --> 00:12:33.260
This smaller grid, by the way, is often called
a kernel.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:36.760
And the beauty here is how just by choosing
a different kernel, you can get different

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:38.170
image processing effect.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:41.170
Not just blurring your edge detection but
also things like sharpening.

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:45.399
For those of you who have heard of a convolutional
neural network, the idea there is to use data

00:12:45.399 --> 00:12:48.570
to figure out what the kernels should be in
the first place.

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:52.960
As determined by whatever the neural network
wants to detect.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:55.580
Another thing I should maybe bring up is the
length of the output.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:59.550
For something like the moving average example,
you might only want to think about the terms

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:01.980
when both of the windows fully align with
each other.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:06.180
Or in the image processing example, maybe
you want the final output to have the same

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:07.740
size as the original.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:12.279
Now, convolutions as a pure math operation,
always produce an array that's bigger than

00:13:12.279 --> 00:13:13.850
the two arrays that you started with.

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:16.860
At least assuming one of them doesn't have
a length of one.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:20.850
Just know that in certain computer science
context, you often want to deliberately truncate

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:25.030
that output.

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:28.610
Another thing worth highlighting is that in
the computer science context, this notion

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:32.790
of flipping around that kernel before you
let it march across the original, often feels

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:35.270
really weird and just uncalled for.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:39.160
But again, note that that's what's inherited
from the pure math context, where like we

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:43.149
saw with the probabilities, it's an incredibly
natural thing to do.

00:13:43.149 --> 00:13:47.100
And actually, I can show you one more pure
math example where even the programmers should

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.690
care about this one because it opens the doors
for much faster algorithm to compute all of

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:52.690
these.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:57.180
To set up what I mean by faster here, let
me go back and pull up some Python again and

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:00.009
I'm going to create two different relatively
big arrays.

00:14:00.009 --> 00:14:05.060
Each one will have 100,000 random elements
in it and I'm going to assess the run time

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:08.260
of the convolve function from the Numpy library.

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:12.110
And in this case, it runs it for multiple
different iterations, tries to find an average

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:16.560
and it looks like on this computer, at least,
it averages at 4.87 seconds.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:22.589
By contrast, if I use a different function
from the SciPy library called FFT convolve,

00:14:22.589 --> 00:14:28.370
which is the same just implemented differently,
that only takes 4.3 milliseconds on average.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:30.980
So, three orders of magnitude improvement.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:34.680
And again, even though it flies under a different
name, it's giving the same output that the

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:40.540
other convolve function does, it's just doing
something to go about it in a cleverer way.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.269
[Music]

00:14:42.269 --> 00:14:46.329
- Remember how with the probability example,
I said another way you could think about the

00:14:46.329 --> 00:14:50.720
convolution was to create this table of all
the pairwise products and then add up those

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:52.970
pairwise products along the diagonals.

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:55.829
There's, of course, nothing specific to probability.

00:14:55.829 --> 00:14:59.640
Anytime you're convolving two different lists
of numbers, you can think about it this way.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:03.759
Create this kind of multiplication table with
all Pairwise products and then each sum along

00:15:03.759 --> 00:15:07.759
the diagonal, corresponds to one of your final
outputs.

00:15:07.759 --> 00:15:11.889
One context where this view is especially
natural, is when you multiply together two

00:15:11.889 --> 00:15:12.980
polynomials.

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:16.920
For example, let me take the little grid we
already have and replace the top terms with

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:24.070
1, 2X and 3X squared and replace the other
terms with 4, 5X and 6X squared.

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:27.670
Now, think about what it means when we're
creating all of these different pairwise products

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:29.579
between the two lists.

00:15:29.579 --> 00:15:33.709
What you're doing is essentially expanding
out the full product of the two polynomials

00:15:33.709 --> 00:15:35.139
I have written down.

00:15:35.139 --> 00:15:40.949
And then when you add up along the diagonal,
that corresponds to collecting all like terms.

00:15:40.949 --> 00:15:44.980
Which is pretty neat, expanding a polynomial
and collecting like terms is exactly the same

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:48.069
process as a convolution.

00:15:48.069 --> 00:15:51.030
But this allows us to do something that's
pretty cool.

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:55.060
Because think about what we're saying here,
we're saying if you take two different functions

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:59.509
and you multiply them together, which is a
simple point wise operation, that's the same

00:15:59.509 --> 00:16:05.459
thing as if you had first extracted the coefficients
from each one of those, assuming they're polynomials,

00:16:05.459 --> 00:16:09.700
and then taking a convolution of those two
lists of coefficients.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:14.250
What makes that so interesting is that convolutions
feel, in principle, a lot more complicated

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:15.970
than simple multiplication.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:20.020
And I don't just mean conceptually they are
harder to think about, I mean computationally

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:24.759
it requires more steps to perform a convolution
than it does to perform a point wise product

00:16:24.759 --> 00:16:25.930
of two different lists.

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:29.750
For example, let's say I gave you two really
big polynomials.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:32.800
Say each one with 100 different coefficients.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:37.100
Then if the way you multiply them was to expand
out this product, you know, filling in this

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:40.640
entire 100X100 grid of pairwise products.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.850
That would require you to perform 10,000 different
products.

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:47.480
And then when you're collecting all the like
terms along the diagonals, that's another

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:50.779
set of around 10,000 operations.

00:16:50.779 --> 00:16:54.819
More generally in the lingo, we'd say the
algorithm is O of N squared.

00:16:54.819 --> 00:16:59.860
Meaning for two lists of size N, the way that
the number of operations scales, is in proportion

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:04.850
to the square of N. On the other hand, if
I think of two polynomials in terms of their

00:17:04.850 --> 00:17:09.319
outputs, for example, sampling their values
at some handful of inputs.

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:13.990
Then multiplying them only requires as many
operations as the number of samples.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:18.539
Since again it's a point wise operation and
with polynomials, you only need finitely mini

00:17:18.539 --> 00:17:20.579
samples to be able to recover the coefficients.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:25.770
For example, two outputs are enough to uniquely
specify a linear polynomial.

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:30.669
Three outputs would be enough to uniquely
specify a quadratic polynomial and in general,

00:17:30.669 --> 00:17:35.500
if you know N distinct outputs, that's enough
to uniquely specify a polynomial that has

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:37.559
N different coefficients.

00:17:37.559 --> 00:17:41.270
Or if you prefer, we could phrase this in
the language of systems of equations.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:44.990
Imagine I tell you, I have some polynomial
but I don't tell you what the coefficients

00:17:44.990 --> 00:17:46.870
are, those are a mystery to you.

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:50.789
In our example, you might think of this as
the product that we're trying to figure out.

00:17:50.789 --> 00:17:54.809
And then suppose I say, I'll just tell you
what the outputs of this polynomial would

00:17:54.809 --> 00:17:59.809
be if you inputted various different inputs
like 0, 1, 2, 3 on and on.

00:17:59.809 --> 00:18:03.679
And I give you enough so that you have as
many equations as you have unknowns.

00:18:03.679 --> 00:18:07.840
It even happens to be a linear system of equations
so that's nice.

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:11.070
And in principle, at least, this should be
enough to recover the coefficients.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:15.440
So the rough algorithm outline then would
be whenever you want to convolve two lists

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:19.549
of numbers, you treat them like they're coefficients
of two polynomials.

00:18:19.549 --> 00:18:25.890
You sample those polynomials at enough outputs,
multiply those samples point wise, and then

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:31.659
solve the system to recover the coefficients
as a sneaky backdoor way to find the convolution.

00:18:31.659 --> 00:18:36.120
And as I've stated it so far, at least, some
of you could rightfully complain, Grant, that

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:38.010
is an idiotic plan.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:41.980
Because for one thing, just calculating all
these samples for one of the polynomials we

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:45.620
know, already takes on the order of N squared
operations.

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:50.090
Not to mention, solving that system is certainly
going to be computationally as difficult as

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:52.590
just doing the convolution in the first place.

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:57.250
So like, sure, we have this connection between
multiplication and convolutions but all of

00:18:57.250 --> 00:19:01.770
the complexity happens in translating from
one viewpoint to the other.

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:03.289
But there is a trick.

00:19:03.289 --> 00:19:07.230
And those of you who know about fourier transforms
and the FFT algorithm, might see where this

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:08.330
is going.

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:11.710
If you're unfamiliar with these topics, what
I'm about to say might seem completely out

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:12.710
of the blue.

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:15.850
Just know that there are certain paths you
could have walked in math that make this more

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:17.540
of an expected step.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.580
Basically the idea is that we have a freedom
of choice here.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:25.680
If instead of evaluating as some arbitrary
set of inputs like 0, 1, 2, 3 on and on, you

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:30.630
choose to evaluate on a very specially selected
set of complex numbers.

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:34.060
Specifically the ones that sit evenly spaced
on the unit circle, what are known as the

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:35.520
roots of unity.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.920
This gives us a friendlier system.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:43.669
The basic idea is that by finding a number
where taking its powers falls into this cycling

00:19:43.669 --> 00:19:47.950
pattern, it means that the system we generate
is going to have a lot of redundancy in the

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:50.100
different terms that you're calculating.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.980
And by being clever about how you leverage
that redundancy, you can save yourself a lot

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.270
of work.

00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:58.770
This set of outputs that I've written has
a special name.

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:02.909
It's called the discrete fourier transform
of the coefficients.

00:20:02.909 --> 00:20:07.049
And if you want to learn more, I actually
did another lecture for that same Julia MIT

00:20:07.049 --> 00:20:09.559
class, all about discrete fourier transforms.

00:20:09.559 --> 00:20:13.150
And there's also a really excellent video
on the channel reducible, talking about the

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:17.610
fast fourier transform, which is an algorithm
for computing these more quickly.

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:22.440
Also Veritasium recently did a really good
video on FFTs so you've got lots of options.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:25.059
And that fast algorithm really is the point
for us.

00:20:25.059 --> 00:20:29.380
Again, because of all this redundancy, there
exist a method to go from the coefficients

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:31.150
to all of these outputs.

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:35.440
Where instead of doing on the order of N squared
operations, you do on the order of N times

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:39.920
the log of N operations which is much much
better as you scale to big lists.

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:42.850
And importantly, this FFT algorithm goes both
ways.

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.659
It also lets you go from the outputs to the
coefficients.

00:20:45.659 --> 00:20:49.240
Though bringing it all together, let's look
back at our algorithm outline.

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:53.210
Now, we can say, whenever you're given two
long lists of numbers and you want to take

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:58.470
their convolution, first, compute the fast
fourier transform of each one of them.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:01.690
Which in the back of your mind, you can just
think of as treating them like they're the

00:21:01.690 --> 00:21:06.960
coefficients of a polynomial and evaluating
it at a very specially selected set of points.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:11.380
Then, multiply together the two results that
you just got, point wise, which is nice and

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:12.380
fast.

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:16.419
And then do an inverse fast fourier transform
and what that gives you is the sneaky back

00:21:16.419 --> 00:21:19.320
door way to compute the convolution that we
were looking for.

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:23.470
But this time, it only involves O of N log
N operations.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:25.210
That's really cool to me.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:30.130
This very specific context where convolutions
show up, multiplying two polynomials, opens

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:33.860
the doors for an algorithm that's relevant
everywhere else where convolutions might come

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:34.860
up.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:38.520
If you want to add probability distributions
to some large image processing, whatever it

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:39.520
might be.

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:42.710
And I just think that's such a good example
of why you should be excited when you see

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:48.950
some operation or concept in math show up
in a lot of seemingly unrelated areas.

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:51.850
If you want a little homework, here's something
that's fun to think about.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:56.610
Explain why when you multi two different numbers,
just ordinary multiplication the way we all

00:21:56.610 --> 00:22:01.159
learn in elementary school, what you're doing
is basically a convolution between the digits

00:22:01.159 --> 00:22:02.669
of those numbers.

00:22:02.669 --> 00:22:07.460
There's some added steps with carries and
the like but the core step is a convolution.

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:12.419
In light of the existence of a fast algorithm,
what that means is if you have two very large

00:22:12.419 --> 00:22:17.010
integers, then there exist a way to find their
product that's faster than the method we learn

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:18.210
in elementary school.

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:23.520
That instead of requiring O of N squared operations,
only requires O of N log N. Which doesn't

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:25.490
even feel like it should be possible.

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:29.350
The catch is that before this is actually
useful in practice, your numbers would have

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:31.650
to be absolutely monstrous.

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:35.310
But still it's cool that such an algorithm
exists.

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:39.039
And next up, will turn our attention to the
continuous case with the special focus on

00:22:39.039 --> 00:22:40.610
probability distributions.

